1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,778
Lepakshi's famous hanging pillar,

2
00:00:01,778 --> 00:00:04,268
is actually not completely in the air.

3
00:00:04,268 --> 00:00:08,536
Investigation found that only its corner touches the ground.

4
00:00:08,536 --> 00:00:12,093
Still, people pass cloth under it.

5
00:00:12,093 --> 00:00:15,650
So is this a miracle, a mistake, or amazing engineering?

6
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:19,150
In Lepakshi village in Andhra Pradesh stands the Veerabhadra temple.

7
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:20,900
In its hall of pillars,

8
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:23,350
the other pillars stand holding up the roof.

9
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:26,150
But among them, this pillar looks different.

10
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:29,300
People say this pillar hangs in the air,

11
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:32,100
as if someone has lifted it up.

12
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:34,589
In this video we will go step by step with evidence.

13
00:00:34,589 --> 00:00:37,078
First, we will see what the scripture says.

14
00:00:37,078 --> 00:00:38,945
Then we will see what the investigation found,

15
00:00:38,945 --> 00:00:40,812
where the experts differ in opinion,

16
00:00:40,812 --> 00:00:43,612
and what nobody knows yet. At the end,

17
00:00:43,612 --> 00:00:45,790
what you can see if you go there.

18
00:00:45,790 --> 00:00:49,801
The story begins with the Skanda Purana. A Purana means an ancient scripture,

19
00:00:49,801 --> 00:00:53,083
in which stories of gods, holy places and sites are written.

20
00:00:53,083 --> 00:00:56,001
Lepakshi's name appears in this scripture,

21
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:58,553
and it appears with great respect.

22
00:00:58,553 --> 00:01:02,200
This is where the foundation of this place's sacredness is laid.

23
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:05,758
According to the Skanda Purana, Lepakshi is one of the divyakshetras.

24
00:01:05,758 --> 00:01:07,735
A divyakshetra means a sacred land,

25
00:01:07,735 --> 00:01:10,898
where the special presence of a god is believed to be.

26
00:01:10,898 --> 00:01:15,247
This place is counted among the important pilgrimage sites of Lord Shiva.

27
00:01:15,247 --> 00:01:18,410
That is why devotees have been coming here for centuries.

28
00:01:18,410 --> 00:01:22,567
This temple is dedicated to Veerabhadra. In the stories of the Puranas, Veerabhadra

29
00:01:22,567 --> 00:01:25,212
is considered a fierce form of Shiv.

30
00:01:25,212 --> 00:01:28,613
In the sanctum, a stone idol is seen from a distance,

31
00:01:28,613 --> 00:01:32,015
in the light of a lamp. Devotees stand with folded hands,

32
00:01:32,015 --> 00:01:34,660
and the air smells of incense sticks.

33
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:37,397
There is a story behind the village's name too.

34
00:01:37,397 --> 00:01:38,766
A folk tale says,

35
00:01:38,766 --> 00:01:41,503
the wounded bird Jatayu from the Ramayan fell here.

36
00:01:41,503 --> 00:01:45,266
Shri Ram said, 'Le Pakshi', meaning rise, bird. That same phrase,

37
00:01:45,266 --> 00:01:49,030
they say, later became the name of this village, Lepakshi.

38
00:01:49,030 --> 00:01:51,940
Now let's come to Virupanna, who got the temple built.

39
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:53,394
According to folk tale,

40
00:01:53,394 --> 00:01:56,304
the king's ministers accused him.

41
00:01:56,304 --> 00:02:00,668
The charge was that they had wasted the royal treasury's money on the temple.

42
00:02:00,668 --> 00:02:03,214
This charge, the story says, was false,

43
00:02:03,214 --> 00:02:05,760
and was made on bad advice.

44
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:07,144
The story goes on to say,

45
00:02:07,144 --> 00:02:10,949
that guessing the punishment, Virupanna took out his own eyes.

46
00:02:10,949 --> 00:02:13,717
He threw them against the temple wall.

47
00:02:13,717 --> 00:02:17,176
Even today, red marks are shown on the wall.

48
00:02:17,176 --> 00:02:20,290
People call them the marks of Virupanna's eyes.

49
00:02:20,290 --> 00:02:24,005
Now let's move from the story to the evidence. The question is simple,

50
00:02:24,005 --> 00:02:26,706
is this pillar really hanging in the air?

51
00:02:26,706 --> 00:02:29,745
For that, we have to look at the pillar carefully.

52
00:02:29,745 --> 00:02:33,460
And understand how this temple and its roof were built.

53
00:02:33,460 --> 00:02:36,498
This temple is from the time of the Vijayanagara empire.

54
00:02:36,498 --> 00:02:40,675
Vijayanagara means the big empire of South India, whose capital was Hampi.

55
00:02:40,675 --> 00:02:44,473
The craftsmen of this empire were famous for their stonework.

56
00:02:44,473 --> 00:02:49,030
Their temples are still known today for their fine carving.

57
00:02:49,030 --> 00:02:50,733
The pillar is made of granite.

58
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:53,118
Granite means a very hard and heavy stone,

59
00:02:53,118 --> 00:02:55,503
which is found plentifully in South India.

60
00:02:55,503 --> 00:02:58,909
Cutting and shaping this stone was not an easy job.

61
00:02:58,909 --> 00:03:00,953
And when the stone is so heavy,

62
00:03:00,953 --> 00:03:04,360
it seems even harder to keep it hanging in the air.

63
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:06,261
This pillar is in the mandap.

64
00:03:06,261 --> 00:03:08,923
A mandap means an open hall resting on pillars,

65
00:03:08,923 --> 00:03:11,204
where people sat and festivals were held.

66
00:03:11,204 --> 00:03:14,626
This mandap at Lepakshi has a lot of pillars.

67
00:03:14,626 --> 00:03:18,049
The pillars have carvings of dancers, musicians and gods,

68
00:03:18,049 --> 00:03:20,330
which look alive.

69
00:03:20,330 --> 00:03:24,205
Now the most important evidence. When the pillar was physically examined,

70
00:03:24,205 --> 00:03:27,305
that is, it was measured and looked at closely,

71
00:03:27,305 --> 00:03:31,567
the picture changed. The pillar is not completely in the air.

72
00:03:31,567 --> 00:03:34,667
Its corner lightly touches the ground.

73
00:03:34,667 --> 00:03:37,380
This is the first key to this secret.

74
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:40,049
Think about it, when you pull a cloth out from underneath,

75
00:03:40,049 --> 00:03:43,384
it slides out from under most of the part.

76
00:03:43,384 --> 00:03:45,719
That's why the pillar seems to be hanging.

77
00:03:45,719 --> 00:03:49,389
But at the corner, the stone and the ground stay in contact.

78
00:03:49,389 --> 00:03:51,390
The illusion comes from right here.

79
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:53,613
The Archaeological Survey of India, that is, ASI,

80
00:03:53,613 --> 00:03:56,577
takes care of the country's old monuments.

81
00:03:56,577 --> 00:04:00,281
The Veerabhadra temple at Lepakshi is also under ASI protection.

82
00:04:00,281 --> 00:04:03,615
That is why breaking or moving the pillar is forbidden today.

83
00:04:03,615 --> 00:04:07,320
Even the examination is done very carefully, without causing any damage.

84
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:11,678
So the biggest proof is that the pillar does not defy gravity.

85
00:04:11,678 --> 00:04:13,019
Gravity means that pull

86
00:04:13,019 --> 00:04:16,036
which draws everything towards the ground.

87
00:04:16,036 --> 00:04:17,713
The pillar does touch the ground,

88
00:04:17,713 --> 00:04:20,730
just by so little that the eye gets fooled.

89
00:04:20,730 --> 00:04:24,211
Now the question is, if the pillar doesn't carry the whole load,

90
00:04:24,211 --> 00:04:25,952
then how does the roof stay up?

91
00:04:25,952 --> 00:04:28,389
The answer is hidden in the structure of the roof.

92
00:04:28,389 --> 00:04:30,478
The roof of this mandap is corbelled,

93
00:04:30,478 --> 00:04:32,567
and built using the dry-stone method.

94
00:04:32,567 --> 00:04:35,700
Let's understand these terms in simple words.

95
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:37,549
Corbel means stone on top of stone,

96
00:04:37,549 --> 00:04:40,940
with each layer placed sticking out a little further. Layer after layer like this,

97
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:43,098
the stones come together in the middle.

98
00:04:43,098 --> 00:04:45,872
This way, instead of the roof's weight going straight down,

99
00:04:45,872 --> 00:04:48,030
it gets spread out onto the supports at the sides.

100
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:51,512
Dry-stone means stones joined without mortar or cement.

101
00:04:51,512 --> 00:04:55,343
No lime, no plaster, just stone sitting properly on stone.

102
00:04:55,343 --> 00:04:58,129
The craftsmen carved the stones so finely

103
00:04:58,129 --> 00:05:01,960
that they locked into each other with their own weight.

104
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:02,612
Now think,

105
00:05:02,612 --> 00:05:06,853
all the pillars of the mandap work together like a connected net.

106
00:05:06,853 --> 00:05:10,114
The roof above spreads the load across all of them.

107
00:05:10,114 --> 00:05:12,397
That is why if one pillar shifts a little,

108
00:05:12,397 --> 00:05:15,007
the whole roof doesn't fall. If the net is strong,

109
00:05:15,007 --> 00:05:17,290
even a weak link stays in place.

110
00:05:17,290 --> 00:05:20,418
This is the real brilliance of the Vijayanagara craftsmen.

111
00:05:20,418 --> 00:05:23,545
They did not have today's computers or machines.

112
00:05:23,545 --> 00:05:27,715
Still, they built a structure that spreads the load evenly.

113
00:05:27,715 --> 00:05:30,148
The hanging pillar is a glimpse of this structure,

114
00:05:30,148 --> 00:05:32,580
which still amazes us today.

115
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:35,886
So how might it have been built? The most likely method is this.

116
00:05:35,886 --> 00:05:37,870
First the pillars were put up,

117
00:05:37,870 --> 00:05:40,845
then the beams and the roof stones were placed on top.

118
00:05:40,845 --> 00:05:44,482
When the roof was fully joined, the load spread across the whole net.

119
00:05:44,482 --> 00:05:49,110
Then even if a pillar is slightly lifted off the ground, the roof doesn't let it fall.

120
00:05:49,110 --> 00:05:51,278
Now comes the most interesting part.

121
00:05:51,278 --> 00:05:53,806
Why is the pillar a little above the ground?

122
00:05:53,806 --> 00:05:56,696
Experts have different opinions on this.

123
00:05:56,696 --> 00:05:59,947
And each side has its own argument. Let's hear

124
00:05:59,947 --> 00:06:03,560
both views with the evidence, without rushing to a decision.

125
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:07,379
Some experts say it was made this way on purpose.

126
00:06:07,379 --> 00:06:09,463
The craftsmen wanted to show off their skill.

127
00:06:09,463 --> 00:06:12,935
They wanted to show that their roof is so strong

128
00:06:12,935 --> 00:06:16,060
that the pillar can stay up even without full support.

129
00:06:16,060 --> 00:06:18,490
So, a wonder that was built deliberately.

130
00:06:18,490 --> 00:06:20,371
Other experts say something different.

131
00:06:20,371 --> 00:06:24,134
According to them, the pillar originally rested fully on the ground.

132
00:06:24,134 --> 00:06:27,897
Then over time the ground beneath it sank unevenly.

133
00:06:27,897 --> 00:06:29,779
This is called foundation settlement,

134
00:06:29,779 --> 00:06:31,660
meaning the ground slowly sinking.

135
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:34,267
This side has more arguments too.

136
00:06:34,267 --> 00:06:37,199
Light earthquake tremors, called seismic tremors,

137
00:06:37,199 --> 00:06:39,154
must have come many times over the centuries.

138
00:06:39,154 --> 00:06:42,738
Such tremors can slowly move even heavy stones.

139
00:06:42,738 --> 00:06:45,670
Maybe that is how the pillar slid out of its place.

140
00:06:45,670 --> 00:06:49,155
So the evidence is the same, but the ways of reading it are different.

141
00:06:49,155 --> 00:06:51,944
Some people believe it was the craftsmen's intention.

142
00:06:51,944 --> 00:06:55,080
Some believe it was the effect of time and the ground.

143
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:59,960
So far, no side has been able to completely prove the others wrong.

144
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:03,299
And we won't give a verdict here. You decide for yourself,

145
00:07:03,299 --> 00:07:05,970
would such skilled craftsmen leave this behind by mistake?

146
00:07:05,970 --> 00:07:09,642
Or did centuries of ground movement and tremors play their game?

147
00:07:09,642 --> 00:07:11,979
Each answer is interesting in itself,

148
00:07:11,979 --> 00:07:14,650
and each answer shows the craftsmen's hard work.

149
00:07:14,650 --> 00:07:17,917
Now, the story that is told the most.

150
00:07:17,917 --> 00:07:20,095
They say that in the time of the British,

151
00:07:20,095 --> 00:07:23,000
a British engineer came to solve this mystery.

152
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,719
He wanted to find out what was really holding up the roof.

153
00:07:27,719 --> 00:07:30,260
And he grabbed the pillar itself.

154
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:31,596
According to the folk tale,

155
00:07:31,596 --> 00:07:34,601
he tried to remove or shake the pillar.

156
00:07:34,601 --> 00:07:36,604
The pillar slipped from its place,

157
00:07:36,604 --> 00:07:40,277
and since then it has been hanging a little slanted. The story says,

158
00:07:40,277 --> 00:07:43,950
the roof began to shake and the engineer got scared and stopped work.

159
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:44,934
But note this,

160
00:07:44,934 --> 00:07:47,884
no solid proof of this story has been found.

161
00:07:47,884 --> 00:07:51,163
Whether any engineer really pierced the pillar or not,

162
00:07:51,163 --> 00:07:53,130
has never been proven to this day.

163
00:07:53,130 --> 00:07:56,081
So we put it in the category of legend,

164
00:07:56,081 --> 00:07:57,720
not in the category of history.

165
00:07:57,720 --> 00:07:59,962
Now let's talk about the questions

166
00:07:59,962 --> 00:08:01,883
whose answers are still incomplete.

167
00:08:01,883 --> 00:08:05,406
Was the pillar hung on purpose, or did it slip later?

168
00:08:05,406 --> 00:08:07,648
The final verdict on this is still pending.

169
00:08:07,648 --> 00:08:10,850
And maybe this puzzle will be solved only when new evidence comes up.

170
00:08:10,850 --> 00:08:12,217
There are more questions.

171
00:08:12,217 --> 00:08:16,319
How much side push can the roof of this mandap withstand,

172
00:08:16,319 --> 00:08:20,078
this has not been fully worked out. Side push means lateral load,

173
00:08:20,078 --> 00:08:24,180
which pushes the stone sideways during an earthquake or strong wind.

174
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:27,695
That is, how much strength the Vijayanagara craftsmen achieved,

175
00:08:27,695 --> 00:08:30,819
has still not been fully measured.

176
00:08:30,819 --> 00:08:33,162
Maybe future research will unlock it.

177
00:08:33,162 --> 00:08:37,068
Until then, this pillar stands as a question, or you could say,

178
00:08:37,068 --> 00:08:38,630
hangs as a question.

179
00:08:38,630 --> 00:08:42,512
Now, something useful for you. If you want to go to Lepakshi,

180
00:08:42,512 --> 00:08:46,041
what should you see? This village is in Andhra Pradesh,

181
00:08:46,041 --> 00:08:48,511
and the temple is still worshipped today. If you go,

182
00:08:48,511 --> 00:08:52,040
keep full respect for the faith and the temple's rules.

183
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:54,371
Go into the mandap and see the hanging pillar.

184
00:08:54,371 --> 00:08:57,868
People still pass cloth under it even today,

185
00:08:57,868 --> 00:09:01,753
and consider it auspicious. It is a tradition of faith.

186
00:09:01,753 --> 00:09:06,027
Just make sure the old stone is not pushed or damaged,

187
00:09:06,027 --> 00:09:07,970
because this heritage belongs to everyone.

188
00:09:07,970 --> 00:09:10,799
Look for the marks of Virupanna's eyes on the wall.

189
00:09:10,799 --> 00:09:13,628
See the colourful paintings on the ceiling and pillars.

190
00:09:13,628 --> 00:09:18,225
And near the temple, be sure to see the huge Nandi statue made of granite,

191
00:09:18,225 --> 00:09:20,700
which can be seen from far away.

192
00:09:20,700 --> 00:09:24,416
So finally, what does the evidence say? The pillar is not in the air,

193
00:09:24,416 --> 00:09:25,767
but it almost hangs.

194
00:09:25,767 --> 00:09:29,145
The weight of the roof is shared across the network of pillars.

195
00:09:29,145 --> 00:09:32,523
And whether this was on purpose or the effect of time,

196
00:09:32,523 --> 00:09:34,550
is still an open question.

197
00:09:34,550 --> 00:09:38,524
Now a direct question for you. What do you think,

198
00:09:38,524 --> 00:09:41,505
was this pillar the craftsmen's intention,

199
00:09:41,505 --> 00:09:45,975
or the effect of the ground settling? Tell us in the comments.

200
00:09:45,975 --> 00:09:49,950
Next time we will go to Hampi, the capital of Vijayanagara,

201
00:09:49,950 --> 00:09:52,433
to uncover the secrets of its stones.
